   "id": "217d10cc",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### 8. Examining your data and determine if a relationship exists between two of the variables. If your data are not suited to that task, determine if a general trend or pattern is present. Write up your findings (250 words)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 20,
   "id": "cba24c4e",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "               Hours_Studied  Exam_Score\n",
      "Hours_Studied       1.000000    0.445455\n",
      "Exam_Score          0.445455    1.000000\n"
     ]
    },
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 640x480 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "print(df[['Hours_Studied','Exam_Score']].corr())\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.scatter(df['Hours_Studied'],df['Exam_Score'])\n",
    "plt.title(\"Relationship between study hours and exam scores\")\n",
    "plt.xlabel(\"Study Hours\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"Exam Score\")\n",
    "plt.show()\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "5075c59d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "- The correlation between study hours and exam scores is about 0.45, indicating a moderate positive linear relationship. This means that students who spend more time studying per week generally achieve higher exam scores. The scatter plot supports this trend, showing a loose upward pattern between the two variables.\n",
    "\n",
    "- However, the relationship is not perfectly linear. Several outliers are visible, some students study many hours but still receive lower scores, while others who study less than 20 hours per week perform exceptionally well even above 90. These irregular points suggest that factors beyond study time, such as motivation, prior knowledge, study efficiency, mental health, or tutoring, also influence exam outcomes.\n",
    "\n",
    "- Although the correlation is positive, it does not imply causation. The data alone cannot prove that studying more causes higher scores. It is possible that highly motivated students both study more and perform better, creating an association that is not purely causal. In addition, the slightly curved and scattered shape of the data points suggests the relationship may be nonlinear, where performance gains eventually level off after a certain amount of study time.\n",
    "\n",
    "- Overall, the analysis shows that while study hours have a meaningful association with exam performance, they are only one of many interacting factors. The presence of outliers, weak linearity, and unmeasured variables indicate that the relationship should be interpreted as correlational rather than causal."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8c142e8a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### 9. Evaluate based upon the current methods how confident you can be with this hypothesis and map out some next steps of how you might go about more credibly (150 words)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2e711165",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "- Based on my current results, I can be moderately confident that there is positive relationship between study hours and exam scores. However, this evidence can only correlational and cannot prove causation. To evaluate the hypothesis, I will apply the hypothesis test using the same logic I learned in econometrics. The null hypothesis (H0) states that the study hours have no effect on exam scores, while the alternative (H1) would state that they do influence. I would test whether the estimated slope in a simple regression is statistically different from zero, using a t-statistic and p-value to decide whether to reject H₀ at a 5% significance level.\n",
    "- To strengthen the analysis, I would estimate the bivariate OLS and multivariate OLS (method controlling other variable returning more accurate estimate of the effect of each independent variable on the final scores) that includes other variables such as previous score, attendance, tutoring sessions. If the coefficient of study hours becomes smaller or less significant in the multivariate model, that would suggest that other interating factors also contribute to the exam performance"
   ]
  },
  {
